# Line endings.
#
# Without this file a Windows checkout (git's default `core.autocrlf=true`)
# writes every text file with CRLF, and the tooling that parses source text
# reads a `\r` the Linux CI never sees. `scripts/gen-catalog.ts` was the first
# casualty: its `GatewayEventType` union regex ends in `;\n`, so on Windows it
# matched nothing and the catalog reported zero declared event types.
#
# LF in the working tree on every platform — the same bytes CI reads.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The two exceptions, which their interpreters require CRLF for: cmd.exe
# mis-parses a multi-line .cmd saved with LF, and PowerShell here-strings are
# line-ending sensitive.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Shell scripts must stay LF even on Windows — `bash` fails on a CRLF shebang.
*.sh text eol=lf
bin/octi text eol=lf

# Never touched.
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.svg text eol=lf
*.lock -diff
package-lock.json -diff
